Now let me also double check: is the causal relation in the conformal completion transitive on M ∪ I+ where q is a boundary point? Causal precedence with endpoint on the boundary: γ(t0) ∈ J⁻(q) means there's a causal curve from γ(t0) to q in M̃. If γ(t) ≺ γ(t0) in M and γ(t0) ≺ q in M̃, then concatenation gives a causal curve from γ(t) to q (the concatenation is continuous, piecewise C¹; causal character preserved). Standard. So r ∈ J⁻(q). Fine.

Note: Some definitions take J⁻(q) to include points connected by *future-directed causal curves*; the concatenation of two future-directed causal curves is a future-directed causal curve. Yes.

Edge case: t0 = 0 → whole curve trivially. Also if t0>0 but the tail is only in J⁻(q) for t>t0 (open tail)? Either convention works; the lemma holds for [t0,T) and (t0,T) alike.

